    ` "из",
    `!"from",
    `""`$ из  (pronounced like  iz ) is a preposition that can be translated into `& as  from    out of   or  by means of   Its usage depends on the context  so it s essential to examine the sentence structure to determine its exact meaning  For example:\n\n1   Из Москвы  - From Moscow (as in location)\n2   Открыл окно из-за стола  - Opened the window from behind the table (as in movement or source)\n3   Читаю книгу  написанную из-под руки  - Reading a book written by hand (as in origin or composition)",
    `#"0.0050307635"
  },
  {
    ` "к",
    `!"towards, toward",
    `"" The Russian letter  к  (K  к) is pronounced similarly to the `&  k  in  kit   In a Russian dictionary  it s often transliterated as  k   making it relatively easy for `& speakers to understand when reading or speaking basic words  However  remember that pronunciation may vary depending on the surrounding letters and syllables  For example  after certain vowels like  а  (a) and  о  (o)   к  may be pronounced slightly softer than when it appears at the beginning of a word or before hard consonants like  т  (t) \n\nTo practice pronunciation  you can use various language learning resources available online or find a native Russian speaker for conversational practice ",
    `#"0.0048074138"
  },
  {
    ` "не",
    `!"but, never, any",
    `""nenul evoy (оценка не равна нулю) is a Russian term that`( non-zero  or  not zero   It is often used in mathematics  statistics  and other fields where numerical values are important  For example  when referring to a number that is not equal to zero  you could use the term  nenul evoy  ",
    `#"0.0046381604"
