  {
    ` "был",
    `!"was",
    `""`$ был  (was) is often translated as  was  or  were  in `&  but when combined with the reflexive pronoun  себя   it forms the past tense of  to be  for oneself  which can be translated as  controlled to be  or  managed to be  in `&  The reflexive verb  был себя  (был себя был) is translated as  was himself  or  managed to be himself   and the negative form is  не был себysя  (не был себя)  A more colloquial version of this phrase  often used in a narrative context  is  был в себе  (был в себе)  which can be translated as  was in oneself  or  managed to hold it together   For example   Он был в себе  means  He managed to keep it together  or  He was himself  in `&  The phrase  являлся  is closely related and translates as  was (being)  or  was serving as   often used to describe someone s past position or function  For example   Этот человек явлсися доктором  means  This person was a doctor  ",
    `#"0.0019957091"
  },
  {
    ` "страницы",
    `!"pages",
    `"" Страницы (stranitsy) is the Russian word for  pages   In a book or document  it refers to individual leaves that make up the whole and are numbered sequentially  It s commonly used in expressions such as  turn to page x  or  read several pages  ",
    `#"0.0019700597"
  },
  {
    ` "до",
    `!"reach, reaching, reaches",
    `""`$ до нельзя  (doneyl zya) is roughly equivalent to  as far as possible  or  to the maximum extent  in `&  It implies doing something to the fullest or reaching a limit  but not necessarily exceeding it  It can also be used to express permission within certain limits  For example  if someone asks  Можно пойти в кино?  (Is it allowed to go to the cinema?)  you might answer  Донельзя  to mean  You are allowed to go  but only under certain conditions or until a specific time  ",
    `#"0.0019363254"
  },
  {
    ` "других",
    `!"other",
    `""`$ других  (drugih) is a plural form of the adjective  другой  (drugoy)  which`( other  or  another  in `&  It is used to refer to people  things  or any other nouns that are not specifically mentioned in the context  implying there are more than what s already discussed or known ",
    `#"0.0017090377"
  },
  {
    ` "была",
    `!"was",
    `""`$ была  (pronounced as  byla ) is the past tense of the verb  есть  (to be) in its feminine singular form  In context  it means  was  or  had been   For example  in the sentence  Эта книга была на столе   which`( This book was on the table    была  corresponds to  was  in `& ",
