  },
  {
    ` "корпуса",
    `!"corps",
    `""`$ корпуса  (pronounced kor-pu-suh) is not related to ships or corpses  as one might initially guess from its similarity to the `& words  corps  or  corpse   Instead  it means  bodies  or  structures   and more specifically in a technical context  it refers to the main part or hull of a ship  In a broader sense  it can also be used to refer to the body or structure of a building or an object  The plural form is  корпуса  (pronounced kor-puh-suh) which when translated into `& is  corpses   as you mentioned  but only in this specific context and not related to ships ",
    `#"8.34645e-05"
  },
  {
    ` "общая",
    `!"total",
    `""`$ общая  (obschaya) is a feminine noun that can be translated to `& as  general  or  common   It is often used to refer to something that belongs  pertains  or affects everyone or many people  such as a general rule  common knowledge  or a shared interest  In some contexts  it may also mean  public   indicating something that is accessible by all members of society ",
    `#"8.33458e-05"
  },
  {
    ` "старая",
    `!"old",
    `""`$ старая  (feminine singular form of  старый ) can be translated into `& as  old woman  or simply  elderly woman   However  it s important to note that this term may not always imply age in the biological sense but rather a more general connotation of an older person  The masculine singular form is  старый   which`( old man  or  elderly man   These words can be used respectfully when referring to older people  although more modern and informal terms for elderly are also common in everyday conversation ",
