    ` "анатолий",
    `!"anatoly, yuri, andrey, leonid",
    `""`$ zet  (Зет) is not a commonly used word in everyday Russian language  However  it can have multiple meanings depending on context:\n\n1  In mathematics  zet denotes the Riemann zeta function  which is a fundamental function in analytic number theory \n2  It could also refer to a specific type of measurement unit  such as a unit of radioactivity or a unit of electrical impedance (Z)  But this usage is not common outside scientific and technical fields \n3  In some Slavic mythology  Zet was the name of a god of wisdom and knowledge \n4   Zet  can also be used informally to refer to someone named Anatoly (Анатолий)  as the first three letters of that name in Russian are А-Н-А (which sounds like the word for  Anatoly  - Анатолий  but if you switch the last two letters around  it becomes А-Н-Т-Л  which can be abbreviated Zet) \n\nIn most cases   zet  is not a word that an `& speaker would encounter in everyday use ",
    `#"5.15159e-05"
  },
