    ` "чарли",
    `!"charlie, billy",
    `#"2.5334e-05",
    `""\"Charly\" is not a standard Russian word as it is an `& name. However, if you're learning Russian and are looking for a way to say \"Charly,\" you would simply use the Latin alphabet version, \"Шарли\" (Sharli). Here, Ш is pronounced like the `& \"sh\" in \"ship\" or \"shoes.\" Enjoy your language learning journey!"
  },
  {
    ` "являлись",
    `!"were, consisted, comprised",
    `#"2.5334e-05",
    `""The Russian word \"были\" (was, were) can be used instead of \"были\" in some contexts. For example, if you want to say \"You were here yesterday,\" you would say \"Вы были здесь вчера\" instead of \"Вы являлись здесь вчера.\" The verb \"быть\" (to be) is conjugated differently depending on the subject, and \"были\" is the past tense of \"быть\" for first person plural (we were)."
  },
  {
    ` "евгения",
    `!"valentina, irina, olga, tatiana",
    `#"2.53279e-05",
    `""The Russian word \"Евгения\"`(\"Eugenia\" in `&. It's a female name of Greek origin, meaning \"well born.\" For those learning Russian, it's important to remember that Russian vowels are pronounced differently compared to their `& counterparts. In this case, the 'e' in \"Евгения\" is pronounced like the 'ye' in \"yes,\" and the 'a' in \"Евгения\" is pronounced as a short 'a', similar to the 'a' in \"cat.\""
  },
  {
