    ` "франциско",
    `!"francisco",
    `#"2.06547e-05",
    `""The Russian word \"франциско\" does not have a standard translation in `& because it is a surname of Spanish origin (Francisco). In Russian, you would typically write it as \"Франциско\" (transcribed phonetically from the Spanish), but if someone is using it as their name, they would be referred to by that spelling. For example, if a person's name were Françoise in French or Francisco in Spanish, their Russian equivalent might be Франциско. To learn Russian names, remember that surnames usually come before given names (unlike in `&). So, you would say \"Франциско Иванович\" (Francisco Ivanovich) if the person's first name is Ivan and they have the patronymic \"Иванович.\""
