    `""In `&, the Russian word \"было\" (which can be translated as \"was\" or \"were\", depending on the context) is often used instead of \"было\" in present-day Russian. However, \"было\" was a past tense form of the verb \"есть\" (to be) and its past participle, \"был(а)\", which are no longer commonly used in standard modern Russian. This particular word, \"которому\", is actually a relative pronoun that means \"which\" or \"that\". If you see it used with \"было\", the correct translation would likely use the past tense of \"be\" (e.g., \"was\" or \"were\") followed by the appropriate article and noun, as in \"That was a book.\" or \"That were books.\""
