  },
  {
    ` "танка",
    `!"tank, tanks",
    `#"1.34872e-05",
    `""Tank (in Russian) is \"танк\" (tank). It's a military armored fighting vehicle designed primarily for the purpose of engaging enemy forces on land."
  },
  {
    ` "обычных",
    `!"fewer, conventional, ordinary, these",
    `""`$ обычных  (obychnych) is a plural masculine noun  It`( ordinary  or  common  in `&  referring to things that are not remarkable  special  or exceptional  It can be used in various contexts  such as describing people  objects  situations  or events  For example  in the sentence  Это обычный дом  (Eto obychny dom)  it`( This is an ordinary/common house  ",
    `#"1.3481e-05"
  },
  {
    ` "генов",
    `!"genes",
    `#"1.3479e-05",
    `""The Russian word \"генов\" is translated as \"genes\" in `&. This term refers to specific sequences of DNA that provide instructions for the development and function of all living organisms."
  },
  {
    ` "камня",
    `!"stone",
    `""`$ камня  (kamnya) is a plural noun that`( stones  or  rocks  in `&  It s important to note that  unlike `&  Russian typically uses the plural form for more than one item  regardless of quantity  For example  two stones would still be kamni (nominative case) ",
    `#"1.3477e-05"
  },
  {
    ` "упразднена",
    `!"abolished",
    `""`$ упразднена  (uprazdnena) is the past participle of the verb  упразднить  (uprazdnit )  which`( to abolish    to eliminate   or  to discontinue  in `&  So  when you see the word  упразднена   it means that something has been officially abolished or discontinued ",
    `#"1.3477e-05"
  },
  {
    ` "монархии",
    `!"monarchy",
    `""`$ монархии  (monarchii)`( monarchy  in `&  It refers to a system of government where one person  often referred to as a monarch  holds supreme power and is typically considered to be a symbol of the state and national unity  In Russia  this term has historical significance as it describes the rule by the Tsars from the establishment of Kievan Rus  in the 9th century until the fall of the Romanov dynasty in 1917 ",
    `#"1.34749e-05"
  },
  {
    ` "свод",
    `!"pillar",
    `""`$ свод  (svod) can be translated to `& as  collection    compilation   or  assemblage   It could also refer to a dome on a building  but that meaning is less common in everyday usage  In the context of a collection  it generally implies a grouping of similar items or ideas  whereas  собрание  (sobranie) is more often used when referring to a gathering of people ",
    `#"1.34749e-05"
