    `#"1.34729e-05"
  },
  {
    ` "взят",
    `!"captured",
    `""`$ взятOM  (transliterated as  vzhyatOM ) can be translated into `& as  taken   but it is most commonly used in the past participle form  взят  (transliterated as  vzyat )  which means  taken  or  seized   It s essential to note that this word changes its ending depending on the gender and number of the noun it is associated with  e g   masculine singular: взял (vzial)  feminine singular: взяла (vziala)  neuter singular: взято (vzyato)  masculine plural: взяли (vzjali)  feminine plural: взяли (vzjali)  and neuter plural: взяты (vzyaty) ",
    `#"1.34708e-05"
  },
  {
    ` "корпорации",
    `!"corporation, corporations",
    `#"1.34708e-05",
    `""Corporation (plural: корпорации) is translated as \"corporations\" in `&. It's a term used to refer to large businesses or companies that are separate legal entities from their owners. In Russian, it can also be used more broadly to describe any organized group or association with a distinct identity."
  },
  {
    ` "гей",
    `!"gay",
    `""`$ гей  (pronounced as  gay ) is a direct translation of the `& term and is used to refer to a homosexual man in Russian language  It s important to note that cultural and societal attitudes towards sexual orientation can differ between countries  so it is always crucial to be sensitive and respectful when discussing or using such terms cross-culturally ",
    `#"1.34688e-05"
  },
  {
    ` "ъ",
    `!"pronounced, tycoon, voiceless, putin",
    `""`$ ym  ( written as  ъ  in older texts) is known as a soft sign or descriptive vowel  It doesn t have any independent meaning but serves a grammatical function  In modern Russian orthography  it s mostly replaced by the letter  ё   Its primary use is to indicate that the next consonant is palatalized (pronounced with the rear part of the tongue touching the hard palate)  For example  the word  дом  (house) without the soft sign becomes  дым  (smoke)  demonstrating the change in consonant pronunciation  It s essential for understanding the phonetics and pronunciation of many Russian words but doesn t correspond to any vowel sound when pronounced ",
    `#"1.34626e-05"
  },
  {
    ` "европой",
    `!"europe",
    `#"1.34585e-05",
    `""Europey in `&`(European (masculine gender)."
  },
  {
    ` "торговле",
    `!"trade",
    `""The Russian word  торговле  (torgovle)`( trade  in `&  It refers to the buying and selling of goods or services  usually between individuals or businesses for profit ",
    `#"1.34565e-05"
  },
  {
    ` "императрицы",
    `!"empress",
    `#"1.34565e-05",
    `""The Russian word \"императрицы\"`(\"Empress\" in `&. It is a title given to a female ruler of an empire. In the context of Russia's history, it was often used as a title for the wife or daughter of an emperor who ruled jointly with her husband or father, and upon their death, she would continue to rule alone as Empress until her own demise."
  },
  {
    ` "самих",
    `!"themselves, these",
    `""`$ самих  (samih) is a plural masculine form of the demonstrative pronoun  самый  (samyj)  which means  the most  the greatest  the best   In the plural form  самих   it`( them  those  when referring to the group that is the most or the best among others  It s essential to remember this word within the context of a sentence to understand its exact meaning ",
    `#"1.34524e-05"
  },
  {
