  {
    ` "обер",
    `!"adjutant, freiherr, von",
    `#"1.30674e-05",
    `""In `&, the Russian word \"обер\" does not have a direct translation because it is used as an imperative form of several verbs in Russian, depending on the context. For example, it can mean:\n\n1. Turn (as in changing direction) - This could be in response to the verb \"орать\" (to plow), where \"обер\" would mean \"turn.\"\n2. Wind (as in a rope or thread) - This could be in response to the verb \"вязать\" (to knit), where \"обер\" would mean \"wind.\"\n3. Overcome, surpass, or outdo - This could be in response to the verb \"преодолевать\" (to overcome), where \"обер\" would mean \"overcome.\"\n4. Wrap, encase, or cover - This could be in response to the verb \"обёртывать\" (to wrap), where \"обер\" would mean \"wrap.\"\n\nTo learn Russian effectively, it's essential to understand that verbs conjugate differently and have various forms depending on the tense, mood, person, and number. So when you encounter a word like \"обер,\" try to figure out its context and look for the corresponding verb in its infinitive form (e.g., \"обходить\" - to walk around, from which \"обер\" could be an imperative form). This way, you'll get more comfortable with understanding and using Russian verbs as you continue learning!"
