    `!"beta",
    `""`$ бета  (beta) is a transliteration of the Greek letter  β  and it has two main uses in Russian  In mathematics  it represents the second partial derivative  In everyday use  it often refers to beta versions or betas tests of software  In the context of finance   Бета-коэффициент  (Beta coefficient) is a statistical measure reflecting the systemic risk of a financial asset or a portfolio in comparison to the market as a whole ",
    `#"1.29507e-05"
  },
  {
    ` "библия",
    `!"bible",
    `""`$ библия  (transliterated as  Bibliya ) is the equivalent of the `& word  Bible   It refers to the holy scriptures of Christianity  which are considered sacred texts by many Christian denominations  In Russia  it s commonly used in the context of the Orthodox Christian faith  where the Bible is a foundational religious text ",
    `#"1.29445e-05"
  },
  {
    ` "ненецкий",
    `!"altai, nunavut, krai, inuit",
    `""Ненецкий (neneckiy in Latin script) is a Russian adjective that originates from the Nenets people  an indigenous Finnic-speaking group living mainly in the Yamal-Nenets Autonomous Okrug and the Arkhangelsk Oblast of northern Russia  The term  Ненецкий  can be used to describe things related to or belonging to the Nenets culture  language  or region  such as a Ненецкий язык (Nenets language) or a Ненецкий район (Nenets district) ",
    `#"1.29445e-05"
  },
  {
    ` "сохранилась",
    `!"survives",
    `""`$ сохранилась  (sokhranilsya) can be translated as  has been preserved  or  remained  in `&  It is used when something  which could have perished or disappeared  has instead persisted or stayed intact  For example  if a historic building was damaged but not destroyed and still stands today  it can be said that it  сохранилась  ",
    `#"1.29445e-05"
