  {
    ` "посвящена",
    `!"dedicated, titled, entitled",
    `#"1.22605e-05",
    `""The Russian word \"посвящена\"`(\"dedicated\" or \"devoted\" in `&. For someone learning Russian, it's important to note that \"посвящен\" (masculine) and \"посвящённая\" (feminine) are used for nouns, while \"посвящается\" (he/she dedicates) and \"посвящена\" (she/it is dedicated) are used as verbs. It's also worth mentioning that in Russian, the accusative case is typically used when referring to a concrete object being dedicated, while the nominative case is used when referring to something abstract. For example, one could say \"Книга посвящена моему отцу\" (The book is dedicated to my father), which uses the accusative case for \"книгу,\" or \"Моя жизнь посвящена искусству\" (My life is dedicated to art), which uses the nominative case for \"моя жизнь.\" Good luck with your Russian language studies!"
