  {
    ` "ай",
    `!"ne, tha, cho, nan",
    `"" In Russian  the word  ай  (pronounced as  a-yi ) is an interjection or exclamation  similar to  oh   or  ah   in `&  It can express surprise  astonishment  admiration  delight  or even a sense of awe  However  it s important to note that its exact meaning can depend on the context and tone in which it s used ",
    `#"9.455e-06"
  },
  {
    ` "получены",
    `!"obtained",
    `""`$ получены  (pronounced po-lu-chyen-y) is a past participle of the verb  получить  (po-luchit )  which means  to get    to receive  or  to obtain   When used as an adjective   получены  implies that something has been received or obtained  For example:  Документы получены  `( The documents have been received/obtained  ",
    `#"9.455e-06"
  },
  {
    ` "пришло",
    `!"came",
    `""`$ пришло  (prishlo) is a verb in the past tense form  which means  it came   In `&  you can use it as a response to the question  Did something come?  or when referring to something that has arrived at a specific place  For example  if someone asks  Did the package come?   you can respond with  Пришло  (prishlo) in Russian ",
    `#"9.455e-06"
  },
  {
    ` "террор",
    `!"terror",
    `"" The Russian term  террор  (terror) has a similar meaning to its `& counterpart  It refers to extreme and violent cruelty  specifically acts intended to create fear or terror in people for political purposes  such as bombings  shootings  or kidnappings  In historical contexts  it can also refer to systematic oppression or repression by authorities ",
    `#"9.455e-06"
  },
  {
    ` "казалось",
    `!"seemed",
    `""`$ казалось  (kazalsia) is translated as  it seemed  or  it appeared  in `&  It is used to express a subjective perception  idea  or hypothesis that may not be true or based on direct evidence  For example:  Я думал  что это книга  но казалось  что это журнал   (I thought it was a book  but it seemed like it was a magazine )",
    `#"9.4529e-06"
  },
  {
    ` "острый",
    `!"acute",
