    ` "женщинам",
    `!"women, sex",
    `#"7.7737e-06",
    `""The Russian word \"женщинам\"`(\"women\" in `&. It's a plural noun that indicates more than one woman. If you want to say \"to a woman,\" the correct translation would be \"женатине\" (женщине) or simply use \"женщине\" without the accusative case ending \"е\" since it implies both singular and plural in this context.\n\nAs you learn Russian, remember that Russian nouns have different genders: feminine, masculine, neuter, and sometimes even plural-only. This can influence their endings when used in sentences. Also, learn the different cases (nominative, accusative, genitive, dative, instrumental, and prepositional) to help you build correct sentences!\n\nGood luck on your language learning journey!"
